Chapter: AnnotationUnderHeader
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Pins that an annotation written directly under a header reaches the
 Document. The style guide shows annotations in exactly that position,
 and prose collection used to discard every column-2 line it met after a
 header, annotation lines included, so only an annotation the top-level
 loop met directly was ever parsed. The annotation below sits under a
 section header with nothing between them, which is the position that
 was silently dropped; one written after a cites line or between two
 definitions always worked, because those tokens end prose collection
 before the annotation is reached.

 The instrument is a hard-realtime annotation rather than a rationale,
 because a rationale changes nothing an observer can see. This one makes
 bump punctual, and a punctual function reports its instruction count as
 CDX6010. Drop the annotation and that code is never emitted, which is
 what the diag sidecar catches. bump carries enough body, and enough call
 sites, to stay out of the inliner -- an inlined function has no emitted
 body to count, so the diagnostic would go missing for a reason that has
 nothing to do with annotations.

Section: Definitions

 @hard-realtime bump 128

  bump : Integer -> Integer
  bump (n) =
    let a = bit-and n 255
    in let b = bit-or a 16
    in min (b + 1) 200

Section: Body

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
    print-line-uni ("bump1=" & show (bump 41))
    print-line-uni ("bump2=" & show (bump 300))
  end
